• Findings

  - High: the CHStone changes for dfadd and dfmul weakened end-to-end verification instead of proving the new %f support
    works. In tests/CHStone/dfadd/main.go:472 and tests/CHStone/dfmul/main.go:426, the per-case (%f) diagnostic was
    removed. But the compiler/backend was also changed to support PrintVerbFloat, so the benchmark no longer exercises
    that path at all. If %f regresses tomorrow, the current CHStone outputs will still pass. That is less robust than
    rewrite case with a hand-written $fwrite string and a 64-bit operand. It does not cover non-stdout $fwrite, multiple
    operands, interaction with signed rewrites, or an actual design-produced print operation. The bug fixed in
    rewriteFwriteArgs was specifically about stdout argument indexing; this test would not catch several neighboring
    regressions.
    snapshots, not executable checks. Examples: tests/CHStone/dfadd/simulation.result.txt:1, tests/CHStone/dfmul/
    simulation.result.txt:1. They are useful for bookkeeping, but they do not add robustness unless regenerated
    consistently by the same tool flow each time.

  Open Questions

  - Was removing (%f) from dfadd/dfmul intended as a permanent benchmark simplification, or only a temporary
    normalization step?
  - Do you want the CHStone float cases to validate the human-readable float formatting too, or only the bit-exact hex/
    expected/output fields?

  Assessment
  The new unit tests are useful, and the results files show the cases were rerun, but the current changes are only
  partially robust. The biggest gap is that the benchmarks no longer validate the newly added %f support end to end.


