─ Worked for 1m 37s ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

• The residual mismatch isn’t the benchmark content now; it’s the print lowering. Non-FSM prints are clocked in
  an always block, so they fire every cycle and duplicate stdout thousands of times. I’m fixing that by routing
  all print-bearing processes through the one-shot FSM print path instead of the free-running print emitter.

• Edited internal/mlir/emitter.go (+3 -4)
    2083  func processNeedsPrintControlFSM(proc *ir.Process) bool {
    2084 -      // Non-FSM lowering emits each print operation directly, ignoring block
    2085 -      // terminators. For multi-block control flow this would execute prints from
    2086 -      // all branches, so route those processes through the FSM lowering.
    2087 -      return processHasPrintOps(proc) && len(proc.Blocks) > 1
    2085 +      // effects execute once per logical control path instead of on every clock.
    2086 +      return processHasPrintOps(proc)
    2087  }

